# Line endings and binary handling.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS
#
# 60 of ~975 tracked text files are stored with CRLF, 42 of them C++ sources
# under src/. That is invisible day to day, and then it is not: any tool that
# rewrites a file wholesale (a bulk edit, `git stash` round-tripping, a Windows
# editor) silently converts the whole file, and a one-line change lands as a
# whole-file diff. It happened twice in one session, to
# src/core/lsdtt_vector_utils.cpp (1 line -> 757) and
# src/cpu/lsdtt_chi_extraction_cpu.cpp (58 lines -> 3284). Both were caught by
# reading the diffstat; neither was caught by the compiler or the tests,
# because the code was correct in both cases.
#
# `text eol=lf` normalises on commit: the repository holds LF, the working tree
# gets whatever the platform wants. Nothing about editing on Windows or WSL2
# changes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APPLYING THIS IS A SEPARATE COMMIT, ON ITS OWN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Adding this file does NOT convert anything. Existing CRLF files stay as they
# are until they are renormalised:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "Normalise line endings to LF (see .gitattributes)"
#
# Do that on a clean tree, in a commit that changes nothing else. It rewrites
# ~60 files, so mixed into other work it would bury the real change - which is
# exactly the failure mode this file exists to prevent. Anyone with uncommitted
# edits in those files should land them first, or the renormalise will conflict.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Default: let git decide what is text, and store text as LF.
* text=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source and documentation - always LF in the repository.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.cpp   text eol=lf
*.hpp   text eol=lf
*.h     text eol=lf
*.cu    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.smk   text eol=lf
*.in    text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEST FIXTURES AND EXPECTED OUTPUTS ARE NOT NORMALISED.
#
# `-text` means "never translate this, in either direction". These files are
# compared byte for byte, and several are pinned by SHA256, so changing a line
# ending changes the pin and the comparison. That is not a hypothetical:
#
#   tests/data/cosmo_oracle.csv   is a file-hash tripwire whose whole purpose
#                                 is to change when the cosmogenic output
#                                 changes. Renormalising it would trip it for a
#                                 reason that has nothing to do with the
#                                 numerics, and the cosmogenic numeric core is
#                                 not to be disturbed without explicit review.
#
#   tests/expected/cosmo/*.csv    reference values from published studies and
#                                 from the CRONUS emulator, compared exactly.
#
# All 15 CRLF-carrying CSVs in the tree are under these two paths. Leaving them
# alone is the entire reason this section exists - renormalising them is the one
# way this change could break something real.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
tests/data/**       -text
tests/expected/**   -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary data. No platform may perform end-of-line translation on these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.nc    binary
*.tif   binary
*.tiff  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.gpkg  binary
*.fgb   binary
*.dem   binary
*.bil   binary
*.flt   binary
*.hdr   binary
*.prj   binary
*.asc   binary
